s, jokes, anything to keep the fear small.\n\nAfterward, Pilot Juna Kael looked at each face on the bridge and said it plainly: 'There's a traitor aboard. I don't know who. But a distress beacon goes to the Kepler Drift — with all of us, or with whoever's left.'"}, {'n': 4, 'title': "The Traitor's Price", 'text': "Pilot Juna Kael laughed for the first time in days. 'All this way,' they said, 'and we were carrying the lock, not the treasure.' Medic Ren Okafor was already calculating the wake-up sequence.\n\nMedic Ren Okafor caught them at the airlock. There was no fight — only the traitor crying that the Void Syndicate held their family. Pilot Juna Kael understood, and hated understanding. Mercy, out here, was a luxury with teeth.\n\nThen a distress beacon spoke — fully, at last. Not a map but a key, and not to a place but to a shield: an ancient defense grid sleeping around the Kepler Drift, the only thing that could stop the Void Syndicate's fleet."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Grid Awakens', 'text': "They reached the Kepler Drift with the Void Syndicate's whole fleet behind them, a wall of iron against the stars. Pilot Juna Kael broadcast on all bands: 'People of the Kepler Drift, wake the grid. Now.'\n\nMedic Ren Okafor fed a distress beacon into the ancient console. For a heartbeat nothing happened — then the sky caught fire, a lattice of light wrapping the Kepler Drift like a held breath. the Void Syndicate's vanguard shattered against it like waves on rock.\n\nSilence, then. The grid humming. the Kepler Drift safe below. Medic Ren Okafor put a hand on Pilot Juna Kael's shoulder and said what everyone was thinking: 'We live.'"}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Home Signal', 'text': "Pilot Juna Kael stood on the hull at sunset, watching the Kepler Drift turn below. courage among the stars — the stars had taught it, and the crew had proved it, and the grid would remember it.\n\nThey landed at the Kepler Drift to crowds and bells. The old voice from the starlog had belonged to the grid's last keeper, dead a hundred years; a distress beacon was their final message. Pilot Juna Kael laid it in the memorial with honors.\n\nThe traitor's family was found and freed; the traitor wept and was forgiven, and signed on again — this time for real. Pilot Juna Kael's rule about second chances held, barely, and gloriously."}]}

