ke eyes.\n\nA mine took their port array. The deck went dark, then red. Navigator Bo Tak was already moving, cable in teeth, splicing by feel. 'Talk to me,' Captain Nix Halden said, and Navigator Bo Tak talked — numbers, jokes, anything to keep the fear small."}, {'n': 4, 'title': "The Traitor's Price", 'text': "Captain Nix Halden let them live, confined to quarters. 'the Void Syndicate trades in hostages,' Captain Nix Halden told the crew. 'We trade in something better.' It was a gamble; the crew's eyes said they knew it.\n\nCaptain Nix Halden laughed for the first time in days. 'All this way,' they said, 'and we were carrying the lock, not the treasure.' Navigator Bo Tak was already calculating the wake-up sequence.\n\nThe traitor showed themselves at the third waypoint: the signal officer, paid in promises by the Void Syndicate. They seized a coded starlog and ran for the escape pod with the Void Syndicate's ships closing in."}, {'n': 5, 'title': 'The Grid Awakens', 'text': "They reached Station Meridian with the Void Syndicate's whole fleet behind them, a wall of iron against the stars. Captain Nix Halden broadcast on all bands: 'People of Station Meridian, wake the grid. Now.'\n\nThe flagship came on anyway, and Captain Nix Halden met it ship to ship above the burning sky. It was the Void Syndicate themselves on the viewscreen, offering one last deal. Captain Nix Halden answered with the engines at full burn.\n\nSilence, then. The grid humming. Station Meridian safe below. Navigator Bo Tak put a hand on Captain Nix Halden's shoulder and said what everyone was thinking: 'We live.'"}, {'n': 6, 'title': 'Home Signal', 'text': "And when the next distress call came — as it always does, out in the drift — the engines were already warm. There are always more skies. There is always Station Meridian, somewhere, needing its heroes.\n\nThey landed at Station Meridian to crowds and bells. The old voice from the starlog had belonged to the grid's last keeper, dead a hundred years; a coded starlog was their final message. Captain Nix Halden laid it in the memorial with honors.\n\nNavigator Bo Tak was offered an admiralty and laughed in the committee's face. 'I have a ship,' they said. It was the highest compliment the crew had ever heard."}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

